<h1>Introduction to JavaScript</h1>

<p>
JavaScript is a <b>high-level programming language</b> developed by
<b>Brendan Eich</b> in <b>1995</b> at <b>Netscape Communications</b>.
Originally, JavaScript was designed for <b>frontend web development</b>.
Today, JavaScript is one of the core technologies of the web along with
<b>HTML</b> and <b>CSS</b>.

Nowadays, JavaScript is also used for <b>backend development</b> using
<b>Node.js</b>.
</p>

<hr>

<h1>Key Features of JavaScript</h1>

<h2>1. Event-Driven Programming Language</h2>

<p>
JavaScript is an <b>event-driven programming language</b>. It responds
to different user actions such as:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Mouse Click</li>
    <li>Mouse Movement</li>
    <li>Keyboard Input</li>
    <li>Form Submission</li>
</ul>

<p>
Event handling helps developers build responsive and interactive web
applications that react to user actions in real time.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>2. Interactivity</h2>

<p>
JavaScript allows developers to create interactive web pages.
It can add features like:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Interactive Forms</li>
    <li>Animations</li>
    <li>Clickable Buttons</li>
    <li>Dynamic Content</li>
</ul>

<p>
These features improve the user experience and make websites more
interactive.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>3. Interpreted Programming Language</h2>

<p>
JavaScript code is interpreted and executed line by line by the
JavaScript engine.
</p>

<p>
Modern JavaScript engines use <b>JIT (Just-In-Time Compilation)</b>,
which improves the overall performance and execution speed.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>4. Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) Support</h2>

<p>
JavaScript supports Object-Oriented Programming concepts such as:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Encapsulation</li>
    <li>Inheritance</li>
    <li>Polymorphism</li>
</ul>

<p>
These concepts help developers write structured, reusable and modular
code.
</p>
<hr>

<h2>5. Dynamic Typing</h2>

<p>
JavaScript is a <b>dynamically typed</b> programming language. This means
you do not need to declare the data type of a variable.
</p>

<p>
A variable can store different types of values during program execution.
For example, a variable can first store a number and later store a string.
</p>

<p>
This feature makes JavaScript flexible and easy to use, but sometimes it
can also cause type-related errors.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>6. Compatibility with HTML and CSS</h2>

<p>
JavaScript works easily with <b>HTML</b> and <b>CSS</b>.
</p>

<p>
It allows developers to:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Manipulate HTML elements (DOM)</li>
    <li>Change CSS styles</li>
    <li>Update page content dynamically</li>
    <li>Create interactive web pages</li>
</ul>

<p>
This integration makes websites more dynamic and user-friendly.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>7. Browser Support</h2>

<p>
JavaScript is supported by all modern web browsers such as:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Google Chrome</li>
    <li>Mozilla Firefox</li>
    <li>Microsoft Edge</li>
    <li>Safari</li>
    <li>Opera</li>
</ul>

<p>
JavaScript runs directly inside the browser without installing any
additional software or plugins.
</p>

<p>
Modern browsers use powerful JavaScript engines like:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Google Chrome → V8 Engine</li>
    <li>Firefox → SpiderMonkey</li>
    <li>Safari → JavaScriptCore</li>
</ul>

<p>
These engines improve the execution speed of JavaScript programs.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>8. Functional Programming Support</h2>

<p>
JavaScript also supports the Functional Programming approach.
</p>

<p>
It provides features such as:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Functions</li>
    <li>Arrow Functions</li>
    <li>Callback Functions</li>
    <li>Higher-Order Functions</li>
    <li>Closures</li>
</ul>

<p>
These features make JavaScript flexible, reusable and suitable for
different coding styles.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>9. Rich Ecosystem and Library Support</h2>

<p>
JavaScript has a large ecosystem of libraries and frameworks.
</p>

<p>
Developers can use millions of packages available through
<b>NPM (Node Package Manager)</b>.
</p>

<p>
Popular JavaScript libraries and frameworks include:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>React</li>
    <li>Angular</li>
    <li>Vue.js</li>
    <li>Next.js</li>
    <li>Express.js</li>
</ul>

<p>
These tools help developers build modern web applications quickly and efficiently.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>10. JSON Support</h2>

<p>
JavaScript provides excellent support for <b>JSON (JavaScript Object Notation)</b>.
</p>

<p>
JSON is widely used to exchange data between web applications,
servers and REST APIs.
</p>

<p>
Because JavaScript works naturally with JSON, it becomes easier to
create, send and receive data in web applications.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Quick Revision</h2>

<ul>
    <li>JavaScript is a High-Level Programming Language.</li>
    <li>Developed by Brendan Eich in 1995.</li>
    <li>Originally created for Frontend Development.</li>
    <li>Now also used for Backend with Node.js.</li>
    <li>Event-Driven Programming Language.</li>
    <li>Supports Object-Oriented Programming.</li>
    <li>Dynamically Typed Language.</li>
    <li>Works with HTML and CSS.</li>
    <li>Supported by all major web browsers.</li>
    <li>Supports Functional Programming.</li>
    <li>Rich ecosystem with React, Angular, Vue.js, Next.js and Express.js.</li>
    <li>Excellent support for JSON and REST APIs.</li>
</ul>
<hr>

<h1>Interview Questions</h1>

<h3>1. What is JavaScript?</h3>
<p>
JavaScript is a high-level, interpreted programming language used to create interactive web pages.
</p>

<h3>2. Who developed JavaScript?</h3>
<p>
Brendan Eich developed JavaScript in 1995 at Netscape Communications.
</p>

<h3>3. Is JavaScript Frontend or Backend?</h3>
<p>
JavaScript is used for both Frontend and Backend development. Backend development is done using Node.js.
</p>

<h3>4. What is Dynamic Typing?</h3>
<p>
Dynamic Typing means a variable can store different types of data without declaring its data type.
</p>

<h3>5. What is JSON?</h3>
<p>
JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data format used to exchange data between client and server.
</p>

